    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" xml:space="preserve">
                <ab>
                 <lb n="1"/><g ref="#crux">†</g> Μνήσθητι <expan><abbr>Κ</abbr><ex>ύρι</ex>ε</expan> τῶν <expan><abbr>σ</abbr><ex>ῶν</ex></expan>
                 <lb n="2"/>δ<hi rend="ligature">ού</hi>λων <persName type="attested" ref="#pers34">Ἀλεξίου</persName> <expan><abbr>κ</abbr><ex>αὶ</ex></expan> <persName type="attested" ref="#pers32">Ἰωά<hi rend="ligature">νν</hi><hi rend="ligature">ου</hi></persName>
                 <lb n="3"/><supplied reason="lost">τ</supplied>ῶν <hi rend="ligature">κτ</hi>ητορων <expan><abbr>κ</abbr><ex>αὶ</ex></expan> <expan><abbr>αὐταδέλφ</abbr><ex>ων</ex></expan>
                 <lb n="4"/><supplied reason="lost">ἐ</supplied>γένετο ἐν <date from="1362-09-01" to="1363-08-31">έτει <num value="6871"><hi rend="supraline">ˏςωοα</hi></num></date>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <p> l. 1: σ[ῶν] Liakos; l. 3: αὐταδέλφ[ων] Liakos (D. Liakos seems to believe that the right part of the slab is missing and that some letters in that part are lost. His edition therefore indicates that that the word endings of lines 1 and 3 were originally inscribed by the stone cutter)</p>
            </div>
            <div type="translation">
                <p></p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary">
                <p>commentary</p>
            </div>
            <div type="bibliography">
                <p>Liakos, Athonite Dedicatory Inscriptions, 177, no. 3.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
